# S20 VoLTE — SELinux policy (Magisk applies this via magiskpolicy at boot).
#
# GOAL: let the Samsung IMS stack run while keeping GLOBAL SELinux ENFORCING, so we never
# flip the global enforce bit (that is what trips Samsung RKP and force-reboots the device).
# Magisk patches the loaded policy the same way it does for its own domains — this does NOT
# touch the global enforce state, so it is RKP-safe.
#
# STAGE 1 (this version): make just the IMS-path domains permissive to "get it running".
#   - system_app     : the imsservice.apk (android.uid.system -> system_app domain)
#   - system_server  : telephony ImsResolver binds the compat ImsService + reads the app
#   - radio          : rild / telephony / multiclientd (runs as uid radio)
#   - init           : the Samsung daemons (imsd/eris/multiclientd/smdexe/connfwexe/ddexe/
#                      at_distributor) are started from /system/etc/init/*.rc with NO seclabel,
#                      so with no domain transition they run in the init domain.
#
# STAGE 2 (after testing): collect /data/local/tmp/s20volte_denials.txt, run audit2allow,
# replace these `permissive` lines with explicit `allow` rules, and delete this note.

permissive system_app
permissive system_server
permissive radio
permissive init
